[s-0115 at 61 min] and then safeguarding some of those standard traditional methods that have been in use for quite some time. Legal firearms, archery equipment, muzzleloaders, standard fishing and rods and reels and things like that. So really, like I said, safeguarding that, putting that into the constitution. Along with that, really some of the more, you know, important things too is what does it not change? Because there is a lot of information in the media space. So it doesn't change the authority of Colorado Parks and Wildlife. They retain full regulatory authority to set seasons, bag limits, method of take restrictions, licensing requirements, all those things.
[s-0116 at 62 min] That remains unchanged. It does not grant permission to trespass, to go on private property. And then it provides exemptions for protected wildlife. So these constitutional protections, it doesn't apply to endangered species or non-gamed species or any wildlife that doesn't fall under the purview of Colorado Parks and Wildlife. And then one of the really important pieces is it doesn't overturn any previously banned practices. So, you know, various bans that were passed in the 1990s regarding trapping or bear hunting practices, it doesn't overturn those or change any of those upon successful passage of Amendment 83.
